WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
Streaming changes the wait people feel.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:06.659
Streaming sends the response as server-sent events while it is generated.

3
00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:15.536
It doesn’t shorten time to finish, but for a person watching, it moves the felt wait from “until done” to “until the first useful words.

4
00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:25.245
” Anthropic’s latency page lists streaming as one of three core strategies, and OpenAI’s guide calls it the most effective way to make users wait less.

5
00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:28.064
Three details make streaming work in practice.

6
00:00:28.344 --> 00:00:29.645
Thinking comes first.

7
00:00:29.925 --> 00:00:46.352
On current Claude models the thinking display defaults to omitted, so a streamed response can show a long silence before text while the model reasons; you can request summarized thinking, or show a status line (“checking your order history…”) driven by tool-call events.

8
00:00:46.632 --> 00:00:48.189
Measure the right timestamps.

9
00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:58.581
Log the time you sent the request, the message_start event (a client-side stand-in for TTFT), the first text delta (first visible output), and the end.

10
00:00:58.861 --> 00:01:00.312
Keep the final message.

11
00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:08.421
The SDK’s streaming helper still gives you the complete message and its usage at the end, so you lose nothing for cost accounting.
